WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.120
The peer review process for journal publication&nbsp;
is essentially a quality control mechanism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.800
It is a process by which experts evaluate&nbsp;
scholarly works, and its objective is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.920
ensure a high quality of published science.&nbsp;
However, peer reviewers do not make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:29.360
decision to accept or reject papers. At most, they&nbsp;
recommend a decision. At peer-reviewed journals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.400
decision-making authority rests solely with&nbsp;
journal editors or the journal’s editorial board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.160
Indeed, it is the journal editor who is considered&nbsp;
to be central to the decision making process.&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.680
Typically, after a paper is submitted&nbsp;
to a journal, a journal editor screens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.520
the manuscript and decides whether or&nbsp;
not to send it for full peer review.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:58.240
Only after clearing the initial screening is the&nbsp;
manuscript sent to one or more peer reviewers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.720
Finally, journal editors or the journal’s&nbsp;
editorial board consider the peer reviewers’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.520
reports and make the final decision to accept&nbsp;
or reject the manuscript for publication.&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.600
Approximately 3 million manuscripts are submitted&nbsp;
to journals every year. Given the large volume of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.320
manuscript submissions, more and more journals&nbsp;
follow a policy of screening papers before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.240
sending them for full peer review. Journal editors&nbsp;
typically look at hundreds of manuscripts a year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.840
One of the first items that editors will look at&nbsp;
is the cover letter, and they may not get further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.360
than the cover letter if the study does not seem&nbsp;
interesting enough. Therefore, it is imperative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.680
that authors craft a well-written cover letter&nbsp;
that highlights the significance and strength of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.320
their research as well as provides a good reason&nbsp;
why the manuscript is a good fit for the journal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.520
Editors will then go through the abstract&nbsp;
and may even skim through the introduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.560
figures and tables, or other sections of the paper&nbsp;
to determine whether the manuscript passes their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.640
quality threshold. During the initial screening,&nbsp;
journal editors mainly check the following :&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.840
Does the manuscript fit the journal’s scope and&nbsp;
aim, and will it be of interest to the readership?&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.520
Is the manuscript of minimum acceptable&nbsp;
quality? Is the content and writing good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.400
enough to make it worth reviewing?
and, Is the manuscript compliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.240
with the journal’s instructions for authors?
If the manuscript clearly lies outside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.880
scope of the journal, then a rapid rejection&nbsp;
allows the author to quickly find and submit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.600
their manuscript to another journal. Peer&nbsp;
reviewers’ time is wasted when they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.720
to spend time evaluating and giving feedback&nbsp;
for a manuscript of clearly inferior quality.&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.400
Once a manuscript clears the initial&nbsp;
screening, it is sent for peer review.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.680
There are three common types of peer&nbsp;
review for journal publication :
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.400
First, single blind, where the names of&nbsp;
reviewers are not revealed to authors. .&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:02.080
Second, Double blind, where the names of reviewers&nbsp;
and authors are not revealed to each other .&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.680
and Third, Open peer review,&nbsp;
where the Names of authors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.040
reviewers are revealed to each other .
 
Generally, a minimum of 2 or up to 6 peer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.720
reviewers are chosen for the peer review. Peer&nbsp;
reviewers are ideally experts in their field.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.480
Journals usually build a pool of peer reviewers&nbsp;
that have a good track record of producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.400
high-quality reviews. Or they may scan the&nbsp;
bibliography to identify potential reviewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.800
or contact researchers they met at conferences and&nbsp;
seminars. Many journals will first ask potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.280
reviewers whether they are willing to review the&nbsp;
manuscript before assigning them as reviewers. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.000
Editors have to be careful to select&nbsp;
reviewers who have sufficient subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.720
matter expertise to do justice to the manuscript.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.440
Therefore, highly technical papers or papers from&nbsp;
niche subject areas may take longer to review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.800
because it may take editors some time&nbsp;
to locate appropriate reviewers.
 &nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.840
Some journals give authors the option of&nbsp;
recommending preferred and non-preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.800
reviewers. Authors would do well to take&nbsp;
advantage of this option if available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.840
as it can expedite the review process, since it&nbsp;
saves the journal time in looking for reviewers. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.080
The peer review is completed once&nbsp;
all the reviewers send the journal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.920
a detailed report with their comments on&nbsp;
the manuscript and their recommendation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.280
Typically, journals ask reviewers to&nbsp;
complete their reviews within 3-4 weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.840
However, few journals have a&nbsp;
mechanism to enforce the deadline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.760
which is why it can be hard to predict how&nbsp;
long the peer review process will take.&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.000
The journal editor or editorial board considers&nbsp;
the feedback provided by the peer reviewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.760
and arrives at a decision. The following are&nbsp;
the most common decisions that are made :
 &nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.040
First, accept without any changes . The journal&nbsp;
will publish the paper in its original form .&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.480
Second, accept with minor revisions .&nbsp;
The journal will publish the paper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:07.200
asks the author to make small corrections .
Third, accept after major revisions . The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.960
journal will publish the paper provided&nbsp;
the authors make the changes suggested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.720
by the reviewers and editors .
Fourth, revise and resubmit . The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.240
journal is willing to reconsider the&nbsp;
paper in another round of decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:27.040
making after the authors make major changes .
And fifth, reject the paper . The journal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.240
will not publish the paper or reconsider it&nbsp;
even if the authors make major revisions .
 &nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.600
The first option, accept without any&nbsp;
changes, is rare. The second decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.520
accept with minor revisions, is typically the&nbsp;
best outcome authors should hope for. Once a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.640
journal rejects a paper outright, authors are&nbsp;
well advised not to resubmit to the same journal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.080
If the journal wanted to reconsider the paper,&nbsp;
they would have issued a conditional rejection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.000
An outright rejection means that&nbsp;
the journal thinks the paper will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.080
not meet its publication standards or&nbsp;
interests even after heavy revisions.&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.600
Editors’ decision-making policies vary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.240
Some reject when even one peer&nbsp;
reviewer recommends rejection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.280
some when the majority recommend rejection, and&nbsp;
some only when all reviewers recommend rejection.
 &nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.560
It is common for peer reviewers to give&nbsp;
conflicting feedback on the same manuscript.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.080
One journal editorial went as far as to&nbsp;
say “Unanimity between reviewers is rare.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.640
In cases of conflicting feedback,&nbsp;
the journal editor may choose to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.160
send the paper to a third reviewer&nbsp;
before arriving at a decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.240
and the author may have to wait longer for&nbsp;
the peer review process to be completed.&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.800
In summary, Because of a&nbsp;
large number of submissions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.160
top-tier journals are often forced to reject even&nbsp;
high quality manuscripts for various reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.400
like a large number of submissions or lack&nbsp;
of fit with the journal’s editorial focus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.560
While reviewers and editors easily agree on&nbsp;
what is clearly not acceptable for publication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.520
deciding what is worthy of&nbsp;
publication is a tougher challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.040
Finally, journal editors make decisions&nbsp;
to accept or reject papers based on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:19.520
opinion of the papers’ publication&nbsp;
worthiness and reviewers’ comments.

